  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday...\nEarly morning surface analysis depicts a broad surface ridge \naxis stretching from Manitoba southward across the Upper Midwest\ninto the lower Missouri Valley. This ridge axis will gradually \nmigrate eastward across the Midwest today. Under weak northwest \nflow aloft, modest cold air advection will trim temperatures \nback near early-October climatological norms, yielding pleasant \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 70s alongside light winds.\n\nAs the surface high slides east toward the Mid-Atlantic coast \nby Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, return low-level \nsoutherly flow will re-establish across central and southeast \nIllinois. This shift will initiate a warming trend, pushing \nafternoon highs into the mid-to-upper 70s on Tuesday and the \nupper 70s to lower 80s by Wednesday afternoon.\n\nLate Week through Weekend...\nAn extended stretch of tranquil, unseasonably warm weather is \nexpected to persist through late week and into the upcoming \nweekend under the influence of a broad upper-level ridge and \nsplit-flow pattern aloft, keeping the best precip chances north \nor south of central Illinois. The next potential opportunity for\nprecipitation arrives late in the weekend into early next week,\nas model guidance suggests tropical remnants may lift \nnortheastward across the Tennessee Valley. While current \nprobabilistic guidance continues to favor precipitation staying \nprimarily south and east of our forecast area, a slight chance \n(15%) remains for precipitation clipping portions of southeast \nIllinois. Trends will continue to be monitored closely.\n\nFire Weather Considerations...\nWhile light ambient winds through the forecast period will \nlimit widespread fire weather concerns, persistent dry weather \nand increasingly low afternoon relative humidity values \n(particularly south of I-72) will lead to progressive drying of \nagricultural fine fuels. With fall harvest operations in full \nswing, additional awareness is needed and extra caution should \nbe used to prevent agricultural equipment from sparking fires in\ndry crop fields.",
